00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.090
Panos?

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.880
PANOS MAVROMMATIS:
Thanks, Reena.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.360
So as Reena mentioned, I'm
the engineering director

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.510
for Google Safe Browsing, which
is a group of teams at Google

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.360
that fights phishing and
malware across the web, email,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.970
as well as our platforms.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.040
I actually joined in
the Safe Browsing team

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.120
as an engineer in 2006,
so I was able to see

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.280
the threat landscape,
as well as our defenses,

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.510
evolve all those years.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:58.010
So today, I am excited to share
some of those trends with you.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.580
For over 10 years, and
for 10 years before that,

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.420
Google has been doing
one thing really well,

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.600
which is to crawl the web.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.200
What our team does is crawl
the web looking for bad sites.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.510
Here, we present how
many new malware sites

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.580
we detect each week
in the blue line,

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.920
and how many new
phishing sites we detect

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.000
each week in the red line.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.940
And this goes back all
the way since 2009.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.820
So first, the good news is
that exploit-based malware

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.750
has been dropping,
particularly since 2016.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.360
The primary reason for that
has been the security work

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.190
that was done by
the awesome Chrome

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:38.360
team over the last decade
that made browsers really more

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.640
secure against exploits.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.820
Features like automatic
updates and sound boxing

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:46.520
of the rendering
engine have basically

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:50.630
made these attacks
extremely hard.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:55.010
I wish I could say the same for
phishing, but as you can see,

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.340
phishing has actually grown
compared to 10 years ago.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:04.710
We still detect over 30,000
new phishing domains each week.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:09.750
And actually, the previous graph
doesn't show the full picture.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.830
The malware exploits
have dropped,

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.890
but malware is still
around and malware still

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:18.970
finds its way to users.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:21.600
It just uses a different
method of delivery.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:27.690
It asks the user to download
and run the file themselves.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:30.420
Last year, we showed twice
as many download warnings

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.520
in Chrome as in 2016.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.320
Even when we look at
software downloads

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.740
from Chrome enterprise
users, one half

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:43.280
of a percent of every
file download that

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.120
could be carrying an
application or other software

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.820
is flagged by Google Safe
Browsing every single day.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:58.870
Talking about scale
and volume of attacks,

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:01.360
Gmail detects and
protects Google users

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.880
from over 100 million
phishing emails each day.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.540
When we look back
at our defenses

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:10.415
against these threats
over the last 10 years,

00:05:10.415 --> 00:05:13.765
we have learned a few lessons.

00:05:13.765 --> 00:05:16.970
I'm sharing them today because
I think they can be helpful

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.220
if you're either building
your own defenses

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:22.460
or choosing a
platform to partner

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:26.830
with that comes with
those defenses built in.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:28.830
The first lesson we learned
is that, very often,

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:31.950
the cost of defenses can be
disproportionately higher

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:34.530
than the cost of attacks.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:37.770
You can imagine why
that's a problem.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.620
To give you an example and
add some color to that,

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.010
evading a classifier
can be done very easily.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.500
For example, you experiment,
change your email a few times.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.020
Change the file a few times
until it's no longer detected

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.638
and goes through.

00:05:50.638 --> 00:05:52.680
If, on the other hand,
you're on the defense side

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.630
and your job is to
build a classifier that

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:58.230
will be evasion-resistant
to every past

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:01.260
and every future attack
that has ever been created

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:06.320
and will be created, you
should not take that job.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:09.900
It is an MP hard problem
proven by academia,

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:11.790
and anyone in the
industry will tell you

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:13.450
that, yes, that's not possible.

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:19.430
Plus, the more targeted the
attack is, the more expensive

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.350
it is to defend against it.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:24.740
High-volume attacks,
they even though they

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:26.450
can reach a lot of
people, they actually

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:29.060
carry a very loud
signal with them,

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:31.700
which is like their volume.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:36.290
So defenders can use volume
to prioritize and defend

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:37.490
against these attacks.

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.740
Low-volume attacks,
on the other side,

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.020
such as spear phishing
or targeted malware,

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:43.850
they tend to fly
under the radar.

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:45.860
And to build classifiers
that catch those

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.864
gets more and more expensive.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:55.460
Machine learning is extremely
important in our industry,

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.552
specifically for security,
especially in this space.

00:06:58.552 --> 00:07:00.260
It can make a big
difference in reducing,

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:04.760
for example, our time to react
to changes automatically.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:09.110
This allows us to build the
most evasion-resistant engines

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:10.550
we can.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:14.280
However, one thing that
we found to be true--

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.490
and we've been using
machine learning since 2008

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:20.060
to classify both phishing
and malware on the web--

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:25.130
is that we really need to
invest in the training data

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:28.100
quality, what we call
ground truth, which

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:32.720
is both the labels
and the data itself,

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.220
the freshness of the data.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:40.260
All of that goes into
the model quality.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.440
And so the lesson here
was that you really

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:45.260
need to invest in the ongoing
process of maintaining

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:47.840
the quality of the data.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:52.910
90%, 95% of your effort will
go into that, as opposed

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:58.560
to picking the latest and
greatest ML algorithm.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.650
User education or
employee training

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:06.240
is something that is common
and it can be useful.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.190
It can reduce the likelihood
that a particular user

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:14.640
may click on a
particular phishing link.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.220
In our experience, this
does not solve the problem.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:23.590
If you think about how
training is delivered often

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:29.050
to employees, we send them
a very original email.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:31.060
Then we ask them
to click on a link.

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:33.669
Then they go through some
kind of authentication dance

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:36.070
and they probably enter
their corporate password

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:39.370
on a site they've
never seen before only

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:42.142
to land on a page that tells
them never to click on emails

00:08:42.142 --> 00:08:44.350
and never to enter their
corporate password on a site

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:47.380
they've never seen before.

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:49.060
And then it tries to
explain to them how

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.550
to pass URLs with their eyes.

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.010
So you need to find
the third slash

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:57.610
and then go back two dots,
sometimes three dots.

00:08:57.610 --> 00:08:59.830
And then that's the
domain you're on.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:01.720
And so URLs were
built for computers,

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:05.080
and even computers have
trouble parsing URLs sometimes.

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.960
How can we expect,
let's say, a nurse

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:14.410
that is finishing their
shift to be able to determine

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:17.440
if a phishing link is
coming from a trusted source

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:20.350
or if the domain
is actually valid?

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:25.840
In general, what we
found is that we're

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:29.560
working with very old
and open ecosystems.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:32.590
The web, email, desktop
operating systems,

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:35.230
these have been so
incredibly successful.

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:39.320
Everyone uses them every
day because they were open.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.570
So we need to do two things.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:44.980
First, work with
these platforms.

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:49.090
We can't just dream of the
future and ignore the present.

00:09:49.090 --> 00:09:52.120
But we also want to build the
platforms of the future that

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.870
have security built into them.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:10:01.400
That's pretty much our
strategy in this space.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:06.380
For building the best
and fastest reactive

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:09.080
and proactive detections
on the platforms

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:13.280
that our users are
on, our best hope

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:17.210
is to leverage our end-to-end
visibility for web and email,

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:20.660
as well as Google scale
to protect the platforms

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:23.930
our users are currently using.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.270
Next, we want to build
the next generation

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:29.750
of platforms, which are actually
designed with security in mind.

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:32.030
I'll give a very
quick example of how

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:33.980
we leverage our visibility
to protect users

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:35.690
on the web and email.

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:39.110
Some Chrome users opt into
sharing security-related threat

00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:40.402
data with Google.

00:10:40.402 --> 00:10:42.110
When Chrome detects
something suspicious,

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:44.270
such as typing your
password on a website

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:47.660
that you've never
been before, Google

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:50.330
is able to quickly
classify these sites.

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:52.310
These could be brand
new phishing sites

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:54.500
that no one has
ever seen before.

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:56.990
If they are
confirmed, then we can

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:58.910
push that data to
Gmail and Gmail

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:01.460
will filter out emails that
contain those links before they

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:04.220
reach the user's inbox.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:08.330
So to summarize, I've shared
the current threat landscape

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:10.490
from Google's unique
vantage point.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:13.550
I've shared some of the lessons
we learned while building

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.350
defenses against them.

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.260
And how critical our
end-to-end visibility has been.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:19.970
Now I want to pass
it back to Reena

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:22.310
to share how would G Suite
and Chrome take advantage

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:25.160
of these technologies, and
provide additional security

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:28.220
features to help you protect
your employees and your users.

00:11:31.785 --> 00:11:33.160
REENA NADKARNI:
Thank you, Panos.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:34.330
That was insightful.

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:41.440
With the advances in malware
and phishing detection,

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:44.980
we believe that a truly
cloud native solution,

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:47.260
like G Suite and
Chrome Enterprise,

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:51.300
can help you secure
your company better.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:56.010
It can improve your
company's security posture.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:57.900
We believe that
we also don't want

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:00.220
to force fit a solution
on our customers.

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:03.000
We want to be able to
meet them where they are.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.580
So today, along with data
that Panos shared, and some

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:07.810
of the insights and
lessons learned,

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:10.230
I'm going to share with you
some of the defenses that we

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:15.330
have to help you in your
journey to the cloud.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:19.230
A simple way to think about
our defenses in G Suite

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:22.200
is that we want them to
be proactive, intelligent,

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:23.690
compliant, and simple.

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:26.910
Let's dive deep into each
of these very quickly.

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:28.470
So proactive.

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:31.230
The admins that manage
these large corporations

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:34.440
are really busy, and
cybersecurity skills

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:36.240
are very hard to come by.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:39.270
So what we want to try to
do is, without the admin

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:42.810
having to do anything at all,
we have proactive defenses

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:43.800
in place.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.620
So things like
polymorphic malware,

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:50.250
things like being able
to detect ransomware

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:52.170
that Panos talked about.

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:57.440
We're very proud of our
record of security on Gmail.

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:59.360
When we think
about intelligence,

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:02.390
we really think about how to
bring better machine learning

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:05.810
insights to be able to show
you all of these alerts

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:08.860
together and easier in one spot.

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:12.950
Our Alert Center allows
admins to manage these alerts

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:16.145
in one spot and take
actions on these alerts.

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:23.020
Last year, we announced the
G Suite Security Center,

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:26.050
and I think it's useful to
dive into that for a second.

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:28.750
The G Suite Security
Center helps an admin

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:32.290
go from prevention,
detection, and remediation

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:34.550
of all of your security issues.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:36.280
So first of all,
before you do anything,

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:38.620
like I said, in
one spot, you get

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:41.710
a complete view of your
organization's security

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:42.760
picture.

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:45.940
We also show you a single
page where we give you

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:48.910
all of G Suite's security
settings in one spot,

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:51.400
and we also show you
if you're deviating

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:54.760
from Google-recommended best
practices in those security

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:57.650
settings, so that's
really helpful.

00:13:57.650 --> 00:14:00.610
And then, finally, we have the
security investigation tool

00:14:00.610 --> 00:14:06.110
that allows you to quickly
remediate these incidences.

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:09.110
But what about compliance?

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:12.830
Google's practices and data
handling practices and such

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:16.620
are audited by third party
auditors across the globe.

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:19.220
So when you come
to Google, we want

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:23.120
to make sure that we are helping
you in your compliance journey.

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:27.320
So for example, the ISO 27001
is a really good example

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.850
of a difficult-to-get
certification.

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:31.220
So we are here.

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:36.000
We're helping you on
your compliance journey.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.630
And then what commitments
do we make to our customers?

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:40.260
Let's be clear about those.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:42.510
First of all, no ads.

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:46.800
Google does not use your data
in G Suite for ads purposes.

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:50.600
Your data does not get scanned
for ads purposes at all.

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:52.860
Secondly, you own your data.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.480
So G Suite customers
can take their data

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:58.810
and leave it anytime
through take out.

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:01.850
Your apps are accessible,
so we have SLAs around that.

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:03.970
And finally, you can
delete your data.

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:06.020
You stay in control
of your own data.

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:10.670
So these are commitments that
we make to our customers.

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:13.670
And security is only as
useful-- all these tools that we

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:16.280
make available to you
are only as useful

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:19.190
as if people really
begin to use these tools.

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:21.480
And so we want to keep
the security tools really,

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:23.600
really simple for our admins.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:25.800
A couple of quick
examples of that.

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:29.090
The first one is that
we have one console--

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:33.770
a single console from which you
can manage all of your admin

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:36.680
security and other--
including your devices--

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:38.810
altogether.

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:42.170
With this centralized
management console,

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:44.840
people tell us that I used to
use this other system before

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:46.460
and it had 14
different consoles.

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:49.790
And we really believe in keeping
things simple for the admins

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:52.160
because they're so overwhelmed
with this amount of data.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.710
Along with our admin
console for G Suite,

00:15:57.710 --> 00:16:01.130
we also get cloud identity
which allows you to bring

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:04.370
in identity from other parties.

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:07.410
And we have our mobile
device management solution,

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:10.280
which has seen a huge amount
of adoption with our customers

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:15.140
both for Android and for iOS,
a mobile device management.

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:17.570
One of my favorite
features of our MDM

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:21.560
is that it allows you to
do agentless monitoring

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:22.590
of devices.

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:25.700
So what that means is if
someone adds your G Suite

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:28.130
account to a device,
without them having

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:31.970
to download anything at all,
your account is protected

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:34.490
and you get access
to enforce a password

00:16:34.490 --> 00:16:36.200
and to do an account wipe.

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:37.700
Not a full device wipe.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:41.030
You don't want to be
deleting baby pictures off

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:42.330
of somebody's phone.

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:44.410
But you can do just a G
Suite account wipe off

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:47.450
of their phone the
minute they add a G Suite

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:48.410
account to the phone.

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:51.380
So that's a really
convenient, very simple way

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:54.830
to get control over
your corporate data.

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:56.980
Data regions.

00:16:56.980 --> 00:16:59.870
So with our geo
disposed data centers,

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:02.270
G Suite actually--
most of our customers

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:04.790
don't want to
regionalize their data.

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:08.420
They enjoy the high
redundancy and latency

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:09.500
that G Suite provides.

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:14.780
However, some of our customers
are under certain regulations

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:20.170
for which they cannot have their
data leave EU or leave the US.

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:23.210
So for that, we
launched data regions.

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:25.430
Really simple, adaptable.

00:17:25.430 --> 00:17:27.020
So if I create an account--

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:30.530
if I create a document
and I am based in EU,

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:32.870
and then I transfer the
ownership of that document

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:35.420
to, say, Panos, who
is based in the US,

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:37.700
automatically, the
data regions policy

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:40.430
will adapt to that
change in ownership.

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:43.580
This year, we are also
including things like backups

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:47.510
and new G Suite services
like forms and sites

00:17:47.510 --> 00:17:49.470
in our data regions offering.

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:53.200
So I'm very excited about that.

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:55.310
There's many customers
that are using

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:58.050
both Chrome enterprise,
as well as G Suite,

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:01.530
to manage their corporate IT.

00:18:01.530 --> 00:18:04.770
We have Veolia, we have
Whirlpool, and others.

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:07.800
But instead of showing you just
these logos, what I thought

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:11.940
would be a really good idea
is for you to hear firsthand,

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:15.810
from a customer, what
their journey was like.

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:19.950
So for that, I'm going
to call Joey Smith.

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:22.840
It's my great honor to
invite Joey on stage.

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:26.040
Joey's the Vice President and
Chief Information Security

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:27.990
Officer for Schnucks.

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:31.560
He was brought in to build
a more mature information

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:36.210
security program in the wake of
a very public and catastrophic

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:37.200
breach.

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:40.120
He's joining us all the way
from St. Louis, Missouri.

00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:41.125
Joey, welcome.

00:18:41.125 --> 00:18:43.500
JOEY J. SMITH: Yeah, as Reena
said, my name's Joey Smith.

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:46.020
I'm the Vice President and
Chief Information Security

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:47.760
Officer for Schnuck Markets.

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:50.220
And I've given this
particular presentation,

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:52.875
or a version of it, in a
number of different settings.

00:18:52.875 --> 00:18:54.750
And I got to tell you,
this is the first time

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:56.730
I've ever done it
in a movie theater,

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:58.973
and you all look
very comfortable.

00:18:58.973 --> 00:19:00.390
So what I want to
talk you through

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:02.460
is a bit about who Schnucks is.

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:05.160
I've met a lot of different
folks over the last two days,

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:08.640
and so far, no one's known
the company that I work for.

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:10.630
And to us, that just
means you're probably

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:11.950
not from the St. Louis area.

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:14.460
Because Schnucks is very
relevant and large in the St.

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:17.100
Louis area, but we
don't have any stores

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:18.210
on the east or west coast.

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:20.790
So let me go a bit
into who Schnucks is

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:23.280
and what it is that we're
doing and, ultimately,

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:25.950
what led us to our decision to
move G Suite, and now further,

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:29.110
as we explore what GCP can
do for us as a business.

00:19:29.110 --> 00:19:32.445
But since I'm going to
talk about a public breach,

00:19:32.445 --> 00:19:34.680
as Reena mentioned,
I do always--

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.780
my attorneys ask
that I open and just

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:39.420
let everyone know that
these opinions are mine.

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:42.700
They're not representative
of my employers.

00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:44.950
This also accounts for an
employer I used to work for.

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:46.290
I used to work for
MasterCard, and they

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.207
used to also have me say
this anytime we go up

00:19:48.207 --> 00:19:51.250
and talk about merchants or
any type of breach event.

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.220
So with that being
said, I somehow now

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:55.800
have the green light to
go give you guys a bit

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:58.680
of a behind-the-curtains view
of what happened to Schnucks

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:00.910
and how we ended up
responding to that.

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:03.120
So first off, let me talk
a bit about Schnuck Markets

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:05.610
and the industry that we're in
and the business that we do.

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:09.390
So Schnuck Markets has been
around for about 79 years now.

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:11.200
We're coming up on
our 80th anniversary.

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:13.540
We were founded
in 1939, and we're

00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:16.780
a third-generation
family-owned grocery retailer.

00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:19.710
We have, at this point-- as
of about six months ago--

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:22.920
we have now about 120
stores across the metro St.

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:25.140
Louis area and
surrounding states--

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:30.960
Missouri, Indiana, Iowa,
Wisconsin, and Illinois.

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:34.330
We employ over 15,000 teammates
across all those stores.

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:36.900
We also have pharmacies
in all of our stores.

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:40.680
We're actually the 48th largest
food retailer in North America,

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:45.420
and the country's 17th largest
privately-held grocery store.

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:48.690
More recently, we've launched
a Schnucks Rewards Program,

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:50.768
a mobile app where you
can now shop and get

00:20:50.768 --> 00:20:52.560
points for all your
purchases, and then you

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:54.700
can cash those
points in for money

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:56.700
off of your current basket
that's going through.

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:59.580
That's been wildly successful,
and we are on track

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:02.760
in the first six to nine months
here to have over a million

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:04.405
of our customers
on that platform.

00:21:04.405 --> 00:21:06.030
So now we're getting
more and more data

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:08.828
that we've never had before
about our customers-- what they

00:21:08.828 --> 00:21:10.620
shop, where they shop,
what they're buying,

00:21:10.620 --> 00:21:13.200
those types of things that we
can leverage tools like Google

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:16.530
Cloud Platform to draw some
very, very powerful insights

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:18.780
into how we can specifically
target those employees

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:22.520
and give them experiences
that they're asking for.

00:21:22.520 --> 00:21:25.400
One of the things that
attracted me to Schnucks

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:28.350
was what it is that they do in
the communities that we serve.

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:29.550
So we're very proud.

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:33.200
We offer our customers what
we call an e-scripts program.

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:36.350
This is a program that you, as a
customer of Schnucks, can go in

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:38.870
and you can set which
local charity you

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:42.560
would like to donate a certain
percentage of your shop to.

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:45.417
So this allows our customers
to pick organizations

00:21:45.417 --> 00:21:46.500
that are relevant to them.

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:49.340
For example, my wife and
I, we pick the school

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:51.860
that our kids go to just
down the road from us.

00:21:51.860 --> 00:21:54.590
And so a certain percentage
of what we spend at Schnucks

00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:57.300
goes directly into the
communities that we serve.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:21:59.960
So it's not necessarily this
big giant corporate initiative,

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:03.520
where maybe the corporate
company has chosen United Way.

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:05.780
And while, as a customer,
I could choose United Way,

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:08.072
I would rather put that more
locally into my community.

00:22:08.072 --> 00:22:11.630
So we're very proud that we
donate more than $2 million

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:13.550
annually to these
types of programs

00:22:13.550 --> 00:22:17.270
across all of St. Louis and
regions that we operate in.

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:19.010
This is, again,
one of the things

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:22.460
that attracted me to Schnucks
because it's something that--

00:22:22.460 --> 00:22:25.430
us giving back really helps us
understand and put our customer

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:28.050
first, which is very
important to us.

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:30.290
So as ingrained
into the community

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:34.640
as Schnucks is, and as everyone
that shops there can appreciate

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:36.890
that they know that
their money is going

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:39.620
to some non-for-profits
that they appreciate,

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:43.310
and while we're all over
the map in St. Louis,

00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:46.740
and as local as we are, like
all of you in the audience,

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:49.070
we also maintain a
global security threat.

00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:52.280
And let me take you back
through a bit of our history,

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:54.740
and really going back
to the year 2013.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:58.070
This was the year
that Symantec named

00:22:58.070 --> 00:22:59.500
the Year of the Mega Breach.

00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:01.208
And I have some of
these numbers up here,

00:23:01.208 --> 00:23:02.840
and I won't read them
out loud, but you

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:05.150
can see this is really the
year that breaches really

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:06.188
started to hockey stick.

00:23:06.188 --> 00:23:07.730
Now you look at some
of these numbers

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:09.810
and compare them to
the numbers today,

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:11.300
and this is really nothing.

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:13.820
But this was the year that
information security was really

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:17.270
put on the map and chief
information security officers

00:23:17.270 --> 00:23:21.325
and cyber security teams started
to get a lot more attention.

00:23:21.325 --> 00:23:23.450
They weren't really in the
back of the bus anymore.

00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:24.825
They were coming
to the forefront

00:23:24.825 --> 00:23:26.400
because all this
stuff was happening.

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:28.550
And at this time, I was
working for MasterCard

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:31.220
and we were doing analytics
on breach merchants

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:34.325
because we were able
to see the fraud data,

00:23:34.325 --> 00:23:36.200
and we could correlate
that to what we called

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:37.610
a common point of purchase.

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:40.217
And we could identify the
merchant that was ultimately

00:23:40.217 --> 00:23:42.800
bleeding the credit card data--
merchants like Target and Home

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:43.412
Depot.

00:23:43.412 --> 00:23:45.620
Well, this was actually
before Target and Home Depot,

00:23:45.620 --> 00:23:51.100
but this was that year wherever
the hackers that were out

00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:53.120
in world started to
recognize and realize

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:55.340
the vulnerabilities
of merchant systems,

00:23:55.340 --> 00:23:57.770
and the lucrative nature of
that magnetic stripe data

00:23:57.770 --> 00:24:00.560
that goes through those systems.

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:06.560
By March 2013, or I should
say, really in December 2012,

00:24:06.560 --> 00:24:09.800
Schnucks fell victim to
one of these statistics.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:13.970
And little did the company
know but in December of 2012,

00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:16.670
a hacker had gained unauthorized
access to our systems,

00:24:16.670 --> 00:24:18.970
put some malware on our
point of sale systems,

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:23.210
and began ciphering credit card
data out of our environment.

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:27.230
It was by March of 2013 that
there was an incredible amount

00:24:27.230 --> 00:24:29.930
of media activity around this.

00:24:29.930 --> 00:24:33.580
The media gives no favors to
merchants that are breached

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:35.460
and really loves to
throw them under the bus.

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:38.390
And it was one of those
catastrophic moments

00:24:38.390 --> 00:24:42.200
in our company's history that
we weren't necessarily prepared

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:45.000
to respond to at the time.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:46.880
So as I think back
again, I was--

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:50.220
St. Louis is a baseball town,
and the Cardinals just started.

00:24:50.220 --> 00:24:51.653
And like all good
St. Louis folks,

00:24:51.653 --> 00:24:53.070
I was sitting on
the couch one day

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:56.130
watching the St. Louis
Cardinals play a game.

00:24:56.130 --> 00:24:58.770
It went to commercial break
and the first commercial

00:24:58.770 --> 00:25:00.840
that popped up was the
CEO of Schnucks sitting

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:04.600
on a chair, suit and tie,
on the edge of tears,

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:06.630
literally taking
commercial time out

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:08.850
to apologize to the
entire city of St. Louis.

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:09.870
I'm sorry, St. Louis.

00:25:09.870 --> 00:25:11.820
We lost your trust.

00:25:11.820 --> 00:25:14.227
We are committed to
winning that trust back.

00:25:14.227 --> 00:25:15.060
It was unbelievable.

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:16.350
You don't see that
type of stuff today.

00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:17.767
So the picture I'm
trying to paint

00:25:17.767 --> 00:25:20.450
is this was before
there was really

00:25:20.450 --> 00:25:24.132
a playbook on how to respond
to these types of events.

00:25:24.132 --> 00:25:25.590
So it was at this
time we were just

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:27.990
seeing this incredible
amount of negative publicity

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:31.133
in the news, constant front
page story of all the local St.

00:25:31.133 --> 00:25:32.300
Louis networks running this.

00:25:32.300 --> 00:25:34.950
We were running commercials
to apologize for this event

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:37.708
and we were absolutely
besides ourselves.

00:25:37.708 --> 00:25:39.750
So as a consumer and just
a customer of Schnucks,

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:40.695
I was absolutely floored.

00:25:40.695 --> 00:25:43.028
And particularly as somebody
in the information security

00:25:43.028 --> 00:25:48.858
field who has been dedicating
their career towards risk

00:25:48.858 --> 00:25:50.400
mitigation and things
of that nature,

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:52.620
I couldn't believe
the amount of press

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:54.473
that Schnucks was getting
around this event.

00:25:54.473 --> 00:25:56.390
And again, I go back to
this was before Target

00:25:56.390 --> 00:25:57.720
and this was before Home Depot.

00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:00.510
And oh, my goodness, Equifax
puts all of these numbers

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:01.560
to shame.

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:04.140
But this was something that
really pushed the company

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:08.200
to the edge of, how are we going
to start to respond to this?

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:10.740
And one of the things
that was interesting to me

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:13.860
as I interviewed for the job
as their first-named Chief

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:16.770
Information Security Officer
was they made a declaration.

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:19.213
They told me, we are going
to look at this event

00:26:19.213 --> 00:26:20.880
and we're not going
to let it define us.

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:23.763
We're going to allow this event
to become our finest hour.

00:26:23.763 --> 00:26:25.680
We're going to learn
from this event and we're

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:27.638
going to use our learnings
to be able to go out

00:26:27.638 --> 00:26:29.700
into the communities that
we serve and further--

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:31.240
like Google Next, for example--

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:33.540
and be able to tell
our story and talk to,

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:36.090
whether it's our customers
or other folks in the IT

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:39.060
and security field about what
we did and how we survived

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:40.480
and how we came out of this.

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:41.730
So let me tell you a
bit about that story

00:26:41.730 --> 00:26:43.355
and, more specifically,
given the theme

00:26:43.355 --> 00:26:46.170
of this conference, what we
ended up doing from a Google

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:48.530
perspective.

00:26:48.530 --> 00:26:52.320
So around this time, I
ended up taking the job.

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:57.120
I began to deploy a risk and
threat-based security strategy

00:26:57.120 --> 00:26:59.730
where we were no longer
just focusing on compliance

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:02.910
and making sure we were PCI
compliant or HIPAA compliant.

00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:06.960
And we began to hire a team
and move people internally

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:09.780
to come over to my
group to really start

00:27:09.780 --> 00:27:13.290
to identify threat and risk and
look at our security posture

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:14.640
through those lens.

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:16.770
At one point, maybe
about a year and a half

00:27:16.770 --> 00:27:20.250
or so into my journey as the
Chief Information Security

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:22.350
Officer there, we
actually did have

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:25.200
an event where a
piece of ransomware

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:27.870
landed on one of our
internal servers.

00:27:27.870 --> 00:27:30.300
And fortunately, because
of some of the security

00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:32.100
controls that we had
been putting in place,

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:33.730
it didn't do anything.

00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:36.220
Our security controls detected
it and it did not install.

00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:37.770
However, it did
breach our perimeter.

00:27:37.770 --> 00:27:40.590
It got in and it landed
on one of our servers.

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:42.318
Mike Kissel's on my team.

00:27:42.318 --> 00:27:44.610
He's the senior manager of
endpoint and cloud security.

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:47.335
Mike came into my office that
day after we looked at that

00:27:47.335 --> 00:27:48.960
and we kind of wiped
our brow and said,

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:50.400
thank god that that
didn't install.

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:51.480
Because had it
installed, it would

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:53.790
have encrypted all of our
unstructured data, which

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:57.346
is where our Microsoft
Exchange server sat

00:27:57.346 --> 00:27:59.075
in that particular data center.

00:27:59.075 --> 00:28:00.450
And he came up to
me and he asked

00:28:00.450 --> 00:28:01.680
me this very serious question.

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:03.097
And this was the
question that was

00:28:03.097 --> 00:28:05.760
a key moment in our
decision as we started

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:08.488
to look at different
ways to protect

00:28:08.488 --> 00:28:09.780
our data and our customer data.

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:10.655
And he asked me this.

00:28:10.655 --> 00:28:12.360
He said, are you
confident, Joey,

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:14.400
enough in this detection
and response strategy

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:15.330
that we've been deploying?

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:17.372
Are you confident enough
that you, right now, can

00:28:17.372 --> 00:28:19.770
walk into our CEO's office--

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:22.080
Mr. Schnucks's
office-- and tell him

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:24.750
that if a ransomware
attack hit our exchange

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:27.996
server that he can fire you?

00:28:27.996 --> 00:28:29.908
I was like, well,
absolutely not.

00:28:29.908 --> 00:28:31.200
We just dodged this one bullet.

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:33.060
We were lucky that our
endpoint controls actually

00:28:33.060 --> 00:28:34.350
detected that and stopped it.

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:36.270
So no, there'd be no
way that I can tell you

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:38.603
that I would be confident
enough to walk into his office

00:28:38.603 --> 00:28:41.250
and say our exchange servers
can survive a ransomware attack.

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:42.080
There's no way.

00:28:42.080 --> 00:28:43.622
And he goes, well
I can tell you how.

00:28:43.622 --> 00:28:45.240
I said, well, let's
start talking.

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:47.370
So this is the beginning
of the introduction

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:50.093
of what Google can mean for
a retailer like Schnucks.

00:28:50.093 --> 00:28:52.260
So we began to look at
Google, and we looked at it--

00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:54.760
obviously, I was looking at it
through the lens of security.

00:28:54.760 --> 00:28:56.580
And what do we
gain by moving some

00:28:56.580 --> 00:28:58.840
of our more valuable
corporate assets and data--

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:00.810
unstructured data,
as well as email,

00:29:00.810 --> 00:29:04.860
calendar, and those types of
things-- into Google Cloud?

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:07.620
Under the lens of
security and looking at,

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:09.567
what's their security
posture look like,

00:29:09.567 --> 00:29:12.150
and what's the funding that they
give to their security teams,

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:13.740
and how do they do encryption?

00:29:13.740 --> 00:29:16.043
Versus all those same
questions on my side.

00:29:16.043 --> 00:29:17.460
And it was the
clear winner that I

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:20.040
can't put in the amount of
security budget or security

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:22.170
stack that a company
like Google can,

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:25.200
a company that's focused
more so on security

00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:28.380
than a local,
privately-held retailer.

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:31.780
So after a lot of collaboration
with the different business

00:29:31.780 --> 00:29:33.780
units and looking at it
through my lens and they

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:36.030
began to look at it through
the lens of collaboration,

00:29:36.030 --> 00:29:39.210
how can we more effectively
run store operations and things

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:41.877
of that nature, all the
other senior leadership

00:29:41.877 --> 00:29:44.460
team at Schnucks ended up making
the decision that G Suite was

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:45.270
the right move to go.

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:47.645
So I'm going to talk now--
and this is where I'll close--

00:29:47.645 --> 00:29:50.200
on the three major
learnings that we have had--

00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:54.030
and we've been on G Suite now
for right over two years--

00:29:54.030 --> 00:29:57.502
and what it is that we learned
in this rollout and some

00:29:57.502 --> 00:29:58.710
of the things that went well.

00:29:58.710 --> 00:30:00.960
Actually, I'll be very honest,
some of the things that

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:02.000
didn't go so well.

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:05.530
And some of the things
that we never expected.

00:30:05.530 --> 00:30:06.413
So first, let's see.

00:30:06.413 --> 00:30:07.330
What went well for us?

00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:10.030
We moved to Google, like
I said, two years ago.

00:30:10.030 --> 00:30:12.340
And prior to doing
that, I talked

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:14.500
about some of the
other senior leaders

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:15.970
that we engaged in this move.

00:30:15.970 --> 00:30:18.970
So it wasn't just another
thing from Joey's team

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:20.980
who's hearing the
word "yes" a lot.

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:22.720
Because we come off
of a breach, they're

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:24.928
going to let me spend the
money that we need to spend

00:30:24.928 --> 00:30:26.608
to help mitigate that risk.

00:30:26.608 --> 00:30:29.150
Let's get the weigh-in from some
of the other business units.

00:30:29.150 --> 00:30:33.130
So we partnered with members
of IT infrastructure, store

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:38.168
operations, digital marketing,
legal, asset protection, HR

00:30:38.168 --> 00:30:40.210
communication, my group
and information security,

00:30:40.210 --> 00:30:42.850
and created a steering committee
around this initiative,

00:30:42.850 --> 00:30:44.830
making sure that what
Google could offer

00:30:44.830 --> 00:30:47.375
us could also help solve
problems and allow them

00:30:47.375 --> 00:30:49.750
to operate the way that they're
used to and, potentially,

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:51.350
even more effectively.

00:30:51.350 --> 00:30:52.330
We started a tech team.

00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:56.290
We issued a very robust
training program around that.

00:30:56.290 --> 00:30:58.480
One of the things that I
loved so much about Google

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:01.210
was literally overnight, once
we turned Google G Suite on,

00:31:01.210 --> 00:31:04.090
I was able to roll out
two-factor authentication

00:31:04.090 --> 00:31:06.400
within just a click of a
button to our end users.

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:07.635
It was absolutely amazing.

00:31:07.635 --> 00:31:09.010
I can't talk about
the importance

00:31:09.010 --> 00:31:10.510
of two-factor
authentication enough,

00:31:10.510 --> 00:31:12.200
from a security perspective.

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:16.150
Google lets me, lets us, do
that absolutely seamlessly.

00:31:16.150 --> 00:31:17.770
It was a beautiful
thing, particularly

00:31:17.770 --> 00:31:19.930
when you look at some of
the other offerings of how

00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:23.750
much they cost and how
much they cost to deploy.

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:27.250
Two-factor just being included
is impressive by Google.

00:31:27.250 --> 00:31:29.470
I thank Google for
that, for sure.

00:31:29.470 --> 00:31:31.270
The immediate
collaboration that we began

00:31:31.270 --> 00:31:32.470
to see across the stores--

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:36.580
I tell this story of we had
a older lady, store manager.

00:31:36.580 --> 00:31:40.150
And her job every Monday morning
was to take an Excel sheet

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:43.967
and put all of the PNLs
across all the stores

00:31:43.967 --> 00:31:45.050
that were in her division.

00:31:45.050 --> 00:31:46.460
So that's maybe like 25 stores.

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:50.590
So she would get her store and
then 24 other email attachments

00:31:50.590 --> 00:31:55.060
of the same stupid Excel
file with their PNLs in it,

00:31:55.060 --> 00:31:57.340
and she'd have to
open every single one,

00:31:57.340 --> 00:31:58.960
Control-C their data.

00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:01.750
Then go to the other one and
Control-V-- copy, paste, copy,

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:03.730
paste 24 other times--

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:04.690
into a master file.

00:32:04.690 --> 00:32:07.017
Save it, attach it, send
it up the chain of command

00:32:07.017 --> 00:32:09.100
to the division manager,
who would then look at it

00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:09.820
and have all that data.

00:32:09.820 --> 00:32:11.210
It was just
absolutely ridiculous

00:32:11.210 --> 00:32:14.710
and it was a very
"Microsofty" way to do things.

00:32:14.710 --> 00:32:16.420
Well, once we rolled
out Google G Suite,

00:32:16.420 --> 00:32:19.975
it was one shared sheet
across that entire division.

00:32:19.975 --> 00:32:21.850
They can now do them on
their mobile devices.

00:32:21.850 --> 00:32:22.600
They can update.

00:32:22.600 --> 00:32:24.610
And one of the coolest
quotes that I got back

00:32:24.610 --> 00:32:27.820
from that group was that
now I can do this-- what

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:30.910
used to take me 20, 30, 40
minutes every Monday morning,

00:32:30.910 --> 00:32:33.970
I can do in five minutes
on my phone on the sales

00:32:33.970 --> 00:32:36.370
floor with my customers where
I need to be, not upstairs

00:32:36.370 --> 00:32:38.500
off of the sales floor.

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:41.140
And the most beautiful
part of that story

00:32:41.140 --> 00:32:43.360
was these weren't
technical teammates.

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:44.850
These are just
teammates that were

00:32:44.850 --> 00:32:46.600
able to figure out
this collaboration tool

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:48.970
and start using it to
get some of the more

00:32:48.970 --> 00:32:52.950
busywork-type stuff out of the
way in a collaborative format.

00:32:52.950 --> 00:32:53.450
Moving on.

00:32:53.450 --> 00:32:54.370
Here's some of the
things that failed.

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:56.090
I talked about two-factor.

00:32:56.090 --> 00:33:00.880
So we are a proud union
employer in the St. Louis area.

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:03.280
The union does have
contracts about you're not

00:33:03.280 --> 00:33:07.280
allowed to use anything
corporate on my personal cell

00:33:07.280 --> 00:33:07.780
phone.

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:09.880
So while you could
probably argue--

00:33:09.880 --> 00:33:12.580
I think us tech-minded people
in the crowd here today,

00:33:12.580 --> 00:33:15.940
we could probably argue that
with Google Authenticator,

00:33:15.940 --> 00:33:18.490
you're not actually
doing any corporate data.

00:33:18.490 --> 00:33:20.620
We're not calling your
AT&amp;T, your Verizon.

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:23.050
We're not charging you for
text messages given those.

00:33:23.050 --> 00:33:25.090
But we couldn't
push out two-factor

00:33:25.090 --> 00:33:27.190
to their personal
devices and that was OK.

00:33:27.190 --> 00:33:30.060
So we were able to solve
that through the UB keys

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:32.490
that we talked about a bit
for anti-phishing and things

00:33:32.490 --> 00:33:32.990
like that.

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:34.540
So we can still
have the two-factor,

00:33:34.540 --> 00:33:36.290
but it wasn't as easy
and wasn't something

00:33:36.290 --> 00:33:40.480
that we necessarily considered,
the union aspect of that.

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:44.530
Limited number of people that
can get into a shared inbox

00:33:44.530 --> 00:33:46.150
has been a bit of
a challenge for us,

00:33:46.150 --> 00:33:50.290
where in a general inbox in
Google, you can share it with--

00:33:50.290 --> 00:33:52.870
I think it's like 20, 25 people.

00:33:52.870 --> 00:33:55.320
Well, our stores have
sometimes over 100 teammates,

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:57.400
so basically, we're left
for the store managers

00:33:57.400 --> 00:33:58.990
to pick their
favorite 25, and those

00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:01.210
are the ones that have
access to that store inbox.

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:03.610
That's an easy fix,
something that we've

00:34:03.610 --> 00:34:05.530
told Google about,
and very simple

00:34:05.530 --> 00:34:07.030
for them to be able to up that.

00:34:07.030 --> 00:34:09.679
We're just waiting
for that new release.

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:10.179
Let's see.

00:34:10.179 --> 00:34:13.750
Executive admins are a
hard group to please.

00:34:13.750 --> 00:34:16.692
So they schedule meetings
and it's different.

00:34:16.692 --> 00:34:19.150
It's one of those things-- we
talk about change management.

00:34:19.150 --> 00:34:20.608
Some of the things
that we did well

00:34:20.608 --> 00:34:22.300
was working with
partners of ours

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:24.405
at Augusto to work through
this change management,

00:34:24.405 --> 00:34:26.572
and how do we address that?

00:34:26.572 --> 00:34:28.780
Make sure you pay attention
to your executive admins.

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:31.719
They are the direct line
to your C level employees,

00:34:31.719 --> 00:34:34.300
and what they say is what your
C level employees believe.

00:34:34.300 --> 00:34:37.060
And if they have a bad time
scheduling an appointment

00:34:37.060 --> 00:34:39.280
because they're not
used to something, then

00:34:39.280 --> 00:34:40.780
ultimately, this
incredible product

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:43.719
can get a negative connotation
and it's not always fair.

00:34:43.719 --> 00:34:46.780
So we did learn that we need
to spend a lot more time

00:34:46.780 --> 00:34:47.877
with our executive admins.

00:34:47.877 --> 00:34:49.960
They're not necessarily
the most technical people.

00:34:49.960 --> 00:34:50.800
They love to listen.

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:52.090
They love to partner with us.

00:34:52.090 --> 00:34:55.073
But at the end of the day, they
are very difficult to please.

00:34:55.073 --> 00:34:57.490
Also with training, we had a
really good training program.

00:34:57.490 --> 00:34:59.830
We made it very simple using
the Google tools for people

00:34:59.830 --> 00:35:00.530
to sign up.

00:35:00.530 --> 00:35:02.030
We did video content.

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:03.100
We recorded the meetings.

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:04.630
We did a lot of the same
things we're seeing here

00:35:04.630 --> 00:35:07.130
with the keynotes, where they
were either streamed live

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:09.190
or you could watch
the video later.

00:35:09.190 --> 00:35:11.690
And then ultimately, just trying
to get people hungry enough

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:13.490
to go seek out that
training on their own

00:35:13.490 --> 00:35:15.980
is also very difficult.

00:35:15.980 --> 00:35:17.990
Things that we never expected.

00:35:17.990 --> 00:35:20.255
It was surprisingly challenging.

00:35:20.255 --> 00:35:22.130
I was mostly excited
about Google, obviously,

00:35:22.130 --> 00:35:23.840
because of the
security wins that I

00:35:23.840 --> 00:35:26.600
get from the two-factor to the
encryption, data in motion,

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:30.830
data at rest, data
out of my environment.

00:35:30.830 --> 00:35:33.530
But it was a lot more
difficult to get our end

00:35:33.530 --> 00:35:37.310
users excited about something
that, to them, was just

00:35:37.310 --> 00:35:41.210
simply perceived as a new
calendar and email tool.

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:43.520
And I say time and
time again, whenever

00:35:43.520 --> 00:35:48.650
I talk to our Schnuck
teammates, that we did not buy

00:35:48.650 --> 00:35:49.910
Google for calendar or email.

00:35:49.910 --> 00:35:51.770
I really, honestly,
don't care that much

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:53.900
about calendar or email.

00:35:53.900 --> 00:35:56.780
Those things are relatively
commodity in my opinion.

00:35:56.780 --> 00:35:58.850
It's all the other tools
that Google G Suite

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:00.770
offers us that empowers
us as employees

00:36:00.770 --> 00:36:04.190
and empowers us as a business,
email and calendar being

00:36:04.190 --> 00:36:05.440
part of that.

00:36:05.440 --> 00:36:08.600
But it was amazingly challenging
to get past the mind of,

00:36:08.600 --> 00:36:10.640
what does drive
mean and how can I

00:36:10.640 --> 00:36:12.750
use Hangouts versus
sending an email?

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:16.160
What is collaboration
in Sheets, Docs, Slides,

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:18.620
and those types of things?

00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:23.090
Really positive ROI on the
reduction of our spam filter

00:36:23.090 --> 00:36:25.340
that used to sit in front
of our exchange environment.

00:36:25.340 --> 00:36:27.757
I know we talked about it a
bit in this session about what

00:36:27.757 --> 00:36:31.580
Google's doing in spam, and
it is, again, just lights-out

00:36:31.580 --> 00:36:34.240
better than any of the
competition that we had seen.

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:37.490
And we did have a spam
provider that we had in front

00:36:37.490 --> 00:36:39.272
of our change environment.

00:36:39.272 --> 00:36:40.730
Once we moved to
Google G Suite, we

00:36:40.730 --> 00:36:43.270
saw that that went quiet
because Google was already

00:36:43.270 --> 00:36:45.770
taking care of it and we were
able to sever our relationship

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:48.050
with that spam
provider, saving us

00:36:48.050 --> 00:36:50.750
a very, very significant amount
of money annually on that.

00:36:50.750 --> 00:36:52.970
We just let Google take
care of the spam for us now.

00:36:52.970 --> 00:36:56.350
Again, all included as part
of the G Suite package.

00:36:56.350 --> 00:36:59.660
I talk about users' technical
knowledge or lack thereof.

00:36:59.660 --> 00:37:01.640
That's not something
to underestimate.

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:03.740
To us, it's very easy.

00:37:03.740 --> 00:37:05.510
You click the blue
button that says send,

00:37:05.510 --> 00:37:06.468
and guess what it does?

00:37:06.468 --> 00:37:07.910
It sends the email.

00:37:07.910 --> 00:37:10.670
That's not always
as understandable

00:37:10.670 --> 00:37:13.550
by some of our end users, and
we were literally coaching them

00:37:13.550 --> 00:37:15.620
through some of what
were take for granted,

00:37:15.620 --> 00:37:18.330
as technically-minded folks.

00:37:18.330 --> 00:37:21.110
So there is a lot of lack
of technical knowledge,

00:37:21.110 --> 00:37:24.260
and it probably is more rooted
in resistance to change,

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:26.000
where--

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:28.430
we've had funny stories where
people are very adamant.

00:37:28.430 --> 00:37:30.890
They don't want to
get out of Excel,

00:37:30.890 --> 00:37:32.203
so they won't go to Sheets.

00:37:32.203 --> 00:37:33.620
And the reasons
that we're hearing

00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:35.840
are just laughable,
where we heard--

00:37:35.840 --> 00:37:37.310
we heard a lady
talk about, I don't

00:37:37.310 --> 00:37:40.180
want to move to Sheets because
I don't trust the math.

00:37:40.180 --> 00:37:42.570
We're like, well, 2 plus
2 still equals 4 in Sheets

00:37:42.570 --> 00:37:43.790
just like it does in Excel.

00:37:43.790 --> 00:37:46.250
So moving-- and help
moving that needle

00:37:46.250 --> 00:37:48.770
from a technical perspective
is also something

00:37:48.770 --> 00:37:50.400
that we didn't expect.

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:53.780
And I talk about Excel and how
deep our love for Excel goes.

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:55.140
And that's very true.

00:37:55.140 --> 00:37:59.960
We have a deep spiderweb
network of Excel files,

00:37:59.960 --> 00:38:02.900
and I imagine that probably
most of you in your businesses

00:38:02.900 --> 00:38:04.280
do as well.

00:38:04.280 --> 00:38:07.370
Google does a great
job of, in most cases,

00:38:07.370 --> 00:38:09.560
transitioning those
Excel files to Sheets.

00:38:09.560 --> 00:38:11.990
What we ask our end users is,
as you create new content,

00:38:11.990 --> 00:38:14.570
create in G Suite and
move forward older stuff.

00:38:14.570 --> 00:38:17.030
But we're moving
further and further away

00:38:17.030 --> 00:38:18.710
from Excel as we're
trying to lessen

00:38:18.710 --> 00:38:19.877
our dependency on Microsoft.

00:38:19.877 --> 00:38:23.390
But it's definitely been
a challenge and something

00:38:23.390 --> 00:38:26.300
that you should definitely keep
in mind as you consider moving

00:38:26.300 --> 00:38:27.500
your business to G Suite.

00:38:27.500 --> 00:38:29.000
So those are some
high-level things.

00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:31.940
What went well, what failed,
and what we never expected.

00:38:31.940 --> 00:38:36.230
This was a decision that we made
in a post-breach environment,

00:38:36.230 --> 00:38:38.480
and Google G Suite has
been a fantastic decision,

00:38:38.480 --> 00:38:41.480
from my perspective as the
information security officer,

00:38:41.480 --> 00:38:44.090
to reduce risk, help
us with threats,

00:38:44.090 --> 00:38:47.240
stop spam, stop
phishing attacks.

00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:49.190
And it's been
something that I've

00:38:49.190 --> 00:38:51.380
been very proud to help
lead that way at Schnucks.

00:38:51.380 --> 00:38:53.900
And it's been one of those
differentiating tools

00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:57.710
as we look to continue to
mitigate our attack service

00:38:57.710 --> 00:39:00.560
and lessen our overall
security risk so

00:39:00.560 --> 00:39:04.820
that those types of events that
we incurred in 2012 and 2013

00:39:04.820 --> 00:39:06.530
never happen again for us.

00:39:06.530 --> 00:39:07.400
So that's our story.

00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:08.510
I appreciate you
guys listening to me.

00:39:08.510 --> 00:39:10.850
Thank you, Google, for having
me out here to share that.

00:39:10.850 --> 00:39:13.225
And Reena, I'll hand it back
over to you to close us out.

00:39:13.225 --> 00:39:14.840
REENA NADKARNI: Thank you, Joey.

00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:17.000
Our mission is to really
change and make work

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:19.650
better for as many
people as possible.

00:39:19.650 --> 00:39:21.830
And so hearing
stories like what Joey

00:39:21.830 --> 00:39:26.438
did with his company's
upgrading to G Suite

00:39:26.438 --> 00:39:27.730
is really heartwarming to hear.

00:39:27.730 --> 00:39:31.010
So thank you for
sharing that story.

00:39:31.010 --> 00:39:33.770
I'm going to next run
through some quick launches,

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:36.290
what's new this year and
what we are launching.

00:39:36.290 --> 00:39:38.300
And then Mitch is going
to show you a quick demo

00:39:38.300 --> 00:39:41.060
and we'll close the
session out, OK?

00:39:41.060 --> 00:39:45.380
So first in our set of launches
is Access Transparency.

00:39:45.380 --> 00:39:49.100
The blog just went out, but
this is an exciting area for us.

00:39:49.100 --> 00:39:52.760
As customers, even large
customers that, in some areas,

00:39:52.760 --> 00:39:55.240
compete with Google
move to G Suite,

00:39:55.240 --> 00:39:57.020
we want to make
sure that they're

00:39:57.020 --> 00:40:01.010
fully comfortable with what
G Suite does and does not

00:40:01.010 --> 00:40:01.850
do with their data.

00:40:01.850 --> 00:40:05.520
We never access a customer's
data for anything other

00:40:05.520 --> 00:40:09.180
than meeting our contractual
obligations to them.

00:40:09.180 --> 00:40:11.730
Sometimes, for example,
when customers call us

00:40:11.730 --> 00:40:14.150
for doing something
like technical support,

00:40:14.150 --> 00:40:16.260
Googlers do need to look
at your data to help

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:18.880
you mitigate those issues.

00:40:18.880 --> 00:40:21.360
But what we're launching
with Access Transparency

00:40:21.360 --> 00:40:25.650
is that every single such
access will be logged

00:40:25.650 --> 00:40:28.320
and you will be able
to file a ticket if you

00:40:28.320 --> 00:40:31.470
have a question about why
someone accessed your data.

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:35.130
And this is industry leading, in
terms of how much transparency

00:40:35.130 --> 00:40:38.040
we are giving you for every
single clear text access

00:40:38.040 --> 00:40:40.000
by a human being at Google.

00:40:40.000 --> 00:40:43.000
So that's Access
Transparency for you.

00:40:43.000 --> 00:40:47.820
The next one is to collaborate
on your security journeys.

00:40:47.820 --> 00:40:51.180
So what we have found is
that most large companies

00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:53.860
have a team of admins
that work together.

00:40:53.860 --> 00:40:56.130
So first of all, we're
giving them the ability

00:40:56.130 --> 00:41:00.810
to create automated alerts,
to take actions-- remediative

00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:03.630
actions on any kind of
alerts that they get.

00:41:03.630 --> 00:41:06.630
And the second thing is
that when you work together

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:09.450
as a team of admins,
you may have--

00:41:09.450 --> 00:41:12.540
the way they collaborate
together is very archaic,

00:41:12.540 --> 00:41:15.930
and we're trying to build G
Suite-style collaboration--

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:18.150
the example that
George just gave--

00:41:18.150 --> 00:41:20.850
even in the admin
security journeys.

00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:25.170
So for example, I come in early
and I have an investigation

00:41:25.170 --> 00:41:26.640
that I am looking at.

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:28.620
And then the next admin
comes in and they want

00:41:28.620 --> 00:41:29.850
to be able to follow along.

00:41:29.850 --> 00:41:32.490
I don't have to take screenshots
and then message them

00:41:32.490 --> 00:41:34.440
to them or email them.

00:41:34.440 --> 00:41:37.500
I can share the investigation
as a view-only investigation,

00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:39.690
and the other admin
can follow along.

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:42.540
This is going to be a
game-changer for our admins

00:41:42.540 --> 00:41:45.600
because no one's really thought
about their user journeys

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:46.840
in this way.

00:41:46.840 --> 00:41:49.920
So I'm very excited about this.

00:41:49.920 --> 00:41:54.157
We also want to provide simple
access anywhere you are.

00:41:54.157 --> 00:41:56.240
So if you're seeing--
there's a theme here, right?

00:41:56.240 --> 00:42:00.140
We want the data to be secure,
but we do not want security

00:42:00.140 --> 00:42:02.810
to get in the way
of your employees

00:42:02.810 --> 00:42:05.600
as they look to be
productive in their day job.

00:42:05.600 --> 00:42:09.140
So context over access is
a huge win in this area.

00:42:09.140 --> 00:42:13.310
What this allows you to do is
that every single access that

00:42:13.310 --> 00:42:17.540
is required for your apps
or your infrastructure

00:42:17.540 --> 00:42:20.840
is vetted specifically
for that access,

00:42:20.840 --> 00:42:23.472
depending on where the person
is, what kind of device

00:42:23.472 --> 00:42:24.930
they're using and
things like that.

00:42:24.930 --> 00:42:27.230
There are many signals
that feed into it,

00:42:27.230 --> 00:42:31.280
and so that context over access
is also launching in beta now.

00:42:31.280 --> 00:42:33.530
And then, like I said
before, for Data Regions,

00:42:33.530 --> 00:42:36.980
we are adding new products,
like forms and sites,

00:42:36.980 --> 00:42:40.580
and we will allow you to
store both your primary data

00:42:40.580 --> 00:42:44.580
and backups in the
place that you want.

00:42:44.580 --> 00:42:48.150
With that, I'm going to call
Mitch Christow, my colleague,

00:42:48.150 --> 00:42:51.100
to come and show you a demo
of some of these technologies.

00:42:51.100 --> 00:42:51.600
Mitch?

00:42:51.600 --> 00:42:52.600
MITCH CHRISTOW: Awesome.

00:42:52.600 --> 00:42:54.210
Thank you, Reena.

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:56.940
Hello, everyone.

00:42:56.940 --> 00:42:59.250
So with the increasing
complexities

00:42:59.250 --> 00:43:02.280
that the corporate
environments are facing

00:43:02.280 --> 00:43:05.430
and the ever-growing
list of threats,

00:43:05.430 --> 00:43:08.530
our security professionals are
really more and more burdened.

00:43:08.530 --> 00:43:11.850
So I want to show you a
couple of examples of products

00:43:11.850 --> 00:43:16.440
and features that Google's
rolling out in order to provide

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:18.900
you with tools
that make it easier

00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:22.740
to use, provide
scalability, and also offer

00:43:22.740 --> 00:43:26.370
best practices on
the security front.

00:43:26.370 --> 00:43:29.650
Can you switch,
please, the input?

00:43:29.650 --> 00:43:31.060
Thank you.

00:43:31.060 --> 00:43:36.750
All right, within the
Security Admin tool,

00:43:36.750 --> 00:43:39.870
for example, you
have the ability now

00:43:39.870 --> 00:43:42.300
to see all of your
security-related settings

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:46.570
on a single page as it pertains
to all of the G Suite settings.

00:43:46.570 --> 00:43:50.280
So with that ease
of use, we give you

00:43:50.280 --> 00:43:53.460
a very simple way of seeing
all of your security settings

00:43:53.460 --> 00:43:56.040
in a single location,
and also follow along

00:43:56.040 --> 00:43:59.700
to see what are the
recommendations that we provide

00:43:59.700 --> 00:44:03.090
for you to improve
the security setup

00:44:03.090 --> 00:44:09.450
or also give you the ability
to see that you've actually

00:44:09.450 --> 00:44:12.400
set up all of the
security-related practices

00:44:12.400 --> 00:44:13.920
that we recommend.

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:16.800
So depending on
the settings, you

00:44:16.800 --> 00:44:19.260
have a very simple
and scalable way

00:44:19.260 --> 00:44:21.120
to actually view
everything that's

00:44:21.120 --> 00:44:24.430
going on within the
resources that you provide.

00:44:24.430 --> 00:44:29.850
Again, this offers all the
administrators a single page

00:44:29.850 --> 00:44:34.890
to see all of your settings,
save time, and centrally manage

00:44:34.890 --> 00:44:38.380
all of your
security-related settings.

00:44:38.380 --> 00:44:40.570
In addition to the
resource settings,

00:44:40.570 --> 00:44:46.407
we also offer you the ability
to manage access via devices.

00:44:46.407 --> 00:44:48.240
More and more people
bring their own devices

00:44:48.240 --> 00:44:51.480
to the corporate
environment, and you

00:44:51.480 --> 00:44:54.630
have to control all
of these devices

00:44:54.630 --> 00:44:58.290
in a scalable and
easy-to-use fashion.

00:44:58.290 --> 00:45:01.200
And what we provide there
is the ability to do agent

00:45:01.200 --> 00:45:03.010
list mobile device management.

00:45:03.010 --> 00:45:06.678
So if somebody shows up
and brings their device,

00:45:06.678 --> 00:45:08.970
you have the ability-- and
I'm going to use here my own

00:45:08.970 --> 00:45:10.590
personal device--

00:45:10.590 --> 00:45:15.000
to connect, for example,
to corporate resources.

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:16.980
And automatically,
without me having

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:18.570
to install anything
on my device,

00:45:18.570 --> 00:45:21.970
I show up within the
device management.

00:45:21.970 --> 00:45:25.470
There is no software to push,
no approvals to perform.

00:45:25.470 --> 00:45:27.810
And I now, as an
administrator, have the ability

00:45:27.810 --> 00:45:30.760
to control that device remotely.

00:45:30.760 --> 00:45:33.930
So again, offering
you the ease of use,

00:45:33.930 --> 00:45:39.000
as well as scalability to
handle any kind of device setup

00:45:39.000 --> 00:45:42.490
that your users may
actually bring to the table.

00:45:42.490 --> 00:45:45.900
And here, you can enforce
all your policies,

00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:50.610
as Reena said earlier, wipe
the data that is specifically

00:45:50.610 --> 00:45:54.250
pertaining to G Suite, or
just disconnect the device

00:45:54.250 --> 00:45:55.317
altogether.

00:46:01.530 --> 00:46:06.030
But within our
environment, you also

00:46:06.030 --> 00:46:10.890
have to control access
way beyond the perimeter

00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:12.570
of your network.

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:15.335
So these days,
people access-- you

00:46:15.335 --> 00:46:17.550
have corporate resources
in any kind of environment

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:18.570
inside of your
corporate network,

00:46:18.570 --> 00:46:19.987
outside of your
corporate network,

00:46:19.987 --> 00:46:21.630
again, with various devices.

00:46:21.630 --> 00:46:25.230
And until now, there have
been very limited capabilities

00:46:25.230 --> 00:46:27.960
on offering fine-grained
and easy-to-use controls

00:46:27.960 --> 00:46:30.130
in those spaces.

00:46:30.130 --> 00:46:32.970
So with that, I want
to introduce to you

00:46:32.970 --> 00:46:34.980
a product that we currently
have in beta, which

00:46:34.980 --> 00:46:40.800
is the Context Over Access that
allows you to manage how people

00:46:40.800 --> 00:46:46.440
are accessing your G Suite
resources with varying

00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:48.662
levels of control.

00:46:48.662 --> 00:46:50.620
So as I said, the product
is currently in beta.

00:46:50.620 --> 00:46:53.590
Some of you may already
be familiar with it.

00:46:53.590 --> 00:46:56.970
But in this case, for
example, a person that

00:46:56.970 --> 00:47:04.950
accesses a Drive resource, you
can enable Context Over Access.

00:47:04.950 --> 00:47:09.270
And by providing
that capability,

00:47:09.270 --> 00:47:12.540
you actually have
the features where

00:47:12.540 --> 00:47:17.130
you can manage access
either by IP subnet device

00:47:17.130 --> 00:47:18.540
policy or access level.

00:47:18.540 --> 00:47:21.870
So depending on the
location of your users

00:47:21.870 --> 00:47:25.980
or the devices that they use,
or the particular access control

00:47:25.980 --> 00:47:29.790
that you have allocated
to them, they now

00:47:29.790 --> 00:47:34.260
can see whether or not
that particular user has

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:37.800
actually access
inside of the network,

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:41.580
as well as outside
of the network.

00:47:41.580 --> 00:47:42.670
OK.

00:47:42.670 --> 00:47:45.400
So just in closing, with
these additional tools

00:47:45.400 --> 00:47:47.950
that we provide, you
can see how we're

00:47:47.950 --> 00:47:50.740
making it easier for you to
control your environment,

00:47:50.740 --> 00:47:54.550
provide you a lot more
control, as well as

00:47:54.550 --> 00:47:57.580
scale these features
with, essentially,

00:47:57.580 --> 00:48:01.480
an always-on default protection.

00:48:01.480 --> 00:48:02.860
Thank you.

00:48:02.860 --> 00:48:07.360
REENA NADKARNI: So to summarize,
Google's strength and security

00:48:07.360 --> 00:48:09.940
really comes from our scale.

00:48:09.940 --> 00:48:13.990
Google carries 1/4 of the
internet's overall traffic.

00:48:13.990 --> 00:48:18.380
With Android and Chrome, as
well as Google Cloud Platform,

00:48:18.380 --> 00:48:20.620
we're able to see
more and hence, we're

00:48:20.620 --> 00:48:22.510
able to build better
machine learning models.

00:48:22.510 --> 00:48:27.080
We're able to better use the
scale to protect our customers.

00:48:27.080 --> 00:48:29.320
We're very focused on
the admin user journeys

00:48:29.320 --> 00:48:32.290
as they go from prevention,
detection, and remediation

00:48:32.290 --> 00:48:34.960
of their security incidences.

00:48:34.960 --> 00:48:37.090
And I'm very grateful for
Panos, Joey, and Mitch

00:48:37.090 --> 00:48:37.840
to be here today.

00:48:37.840 --> 00:48:39.298
We're all going to
be waiting here.

00:48:39.298 --> 00:48:41.440
If you have any questions,
please come back to us

00:48:41.440 --> 00:48:42.440
afterwards.

00:48:42.440 --> 00:48:44.260
With that, thank you.

00:48:44.260 --> 00:48:47.610
[MUSIC PLAYING]

